## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests practical container containment, SELinux policy implementation, audit trail configuration, and host isolation in a realistic data ingestion workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Podman user namespaces for UID/GID isolation
- SELinux targeted policy modules for domain confinement
- Auditd path monitoring for comprehensive file I/O tracking
- Secure bind mounts with minimal host filesystem exposure

## Code Example
```bash
# Create SELinux module
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

# Policy rules for ingest_t domain
allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
```

## Implementation Commands
```bash
# Create dedicated user
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/false ingest
sudo usermod -v 100000-199999 -w 100000-199999 ingest

# Build and load SELinux module
cat > ingest.te << 'EOF'
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
EOF

checkmodule -M -m -o ingest.mod ingest.te
semodule_package -o ingest.pp -m ingest.mod
sudo semodule -i ingest.pp

# Configure audit rules
sudo auditctl -w /opt/ingest/config -p r -k ingest_config
sudo auditctl -w /srv/ingest/data -p rwxa -k ingest_data
sudo auditctl -w /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock -p w -k ingest_ipc

# Run container
podman run \
  --uidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  --gidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  -v /opt/ingest/config:/opt/ingest/config:ro \
  -v /srv/ingest/data:/srv/ingest/data:rw \
  -v /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock:/var/run/ingest-proxy.sock \
  --network=none \
  --security-opt label=type:ingest_t \
  --user 1000 \
  ingest-service:latest
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses end-to-end TLS termination, dynamic cert provisioning, and zero-downtime reload in a Podman-based RH9 setup. Also tests firewall (nftables), SELinux, and service upgrade discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Podman, nginx as TLS terminator, Vault integration, in-container watcher, systemd upgrades, nftables, SELinux, SNI routing

## Code Example
```nginx
# tls_preread for SNI, per-tenant certs loaded from /etc/nginx/certs
stream {
  map $ssl_preread_server_name $tenant {
    tenantA backendA;
    tenantB backendB;
    tenantC backendC;
  }
  server {
    listen 8443;
    ssl_preread on;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Vault outage and certificate revocation?
- How to validate zero-downtime during upgrades?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical application of containerization, SELinux, and deployment orchestration on a single host with zero-downtime goals.

## Key Concepts
- Podman pod lifecycle and per-service user mapping
- SELinux policy customization for containerized apps
- Nginx TLS termination and blue/green traffic shifting
- Data migration, health checks, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```bash
# Podman setup (illustrative)
sudo useradd -r apiuser
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/api/data
podman pod create --name api-pod -p 8443:8443
podman run -d --name api --pod api-pod -v /var/lib/api/data:/data:Z --user apiuser:apiuser myrepo/api:latest
```

```selinux
# Minimal policy snippet outline (illustrative, not full policy)
module api_t 1.0;
require {
  type container_runtime_t;
  type api_data_t;
  class file { read write getattr };
  class dir { write read getattr };
  type self;
  class net_bind_service;
}
allow api_t api_data_t:dir { write read getattr };
allow api_t api_data_t:file { read write getattr };
# Allow binding to 8443 via runtime role
allow api_t self:net_bind_service;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multiple hosts with a shared data backend and zero-downtime failover?
- What observability and metrics would you add to detect drift between blue/green environments?

